WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.542
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We're excited
to share a preview of version

00:00:02.542 --> 00:00:04.450
4 of the Job Search API.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.690
This is just a beta
preview, so everything

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.109
is subject to change.

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:10.480
That being said, there's
some cool stuff coming up,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.410
so let's dive into it.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.715
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.290
Our previous code samples
were all using version 3,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.770
so they'll still work if you
want to start from there.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.780
As always, the
documentation has all

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.330
of the up-to-date information
on which versions are available

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.860
and how to use them.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.960
Version 4 is currently
in beta and has

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.549
some cool new advantages
that you can use.

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:35.540
First up-- there's now the
ability to use tenants.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.600
Tenants are entities that let
you group companies and jobs,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.780
instead of having everything
grouped under your GCP project.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.060
This way, you can
easily isolate tenants

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.150
without having to create new
GCP projects, especially useful

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.120
for things like hiring
agency and job sites working

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.830
with multiple organizations.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:54.560
By default, you'll have a tenant
ID when you set up a Job Search

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:55.530
API.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.470
So if you don't want
to worry about tenants,

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:58.700
you don't have to.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.020
You can continue to use the API
in the same way as version 3,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.580
and it'll always assume
the default tenant.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.650
You can also create a
new tenant with the API,

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.758
then use that for everything
by explicitly specifying it.

00:01:09.758 --> 00:01:11.300
You can create as
many tenants as you

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.670
need to organize your
companies and jobs.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.340
If you're just looking for
your default tenant ID,

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.950
just create a company
without specifying a tenant,

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.990
and you'll be able to
find the ID in the newly

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.060
created company's name.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.130
Besides tenants, there's
also some new features

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.660
around the commute search.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.400
The Job Search API
lets you search

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.320
by a user's preferred
commute time

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.480
by driving and public transport.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.450
Version 4 adds walking
and biking to this search,

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.910
so you can give your users
an even better experience

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.710
when just looking for jobs.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.750
Just include a
commute filter object

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.940
when making a search request
to filter the results

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.320
by the commute parameter.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.750
There's more specifics
in the documentation.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.760
Just as important as the
user search experience

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.760
is your experience
implementing the API.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.590
That's why the
event handling API

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.650
has been created to help make
the client event system easier

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.555
to integrate.

00:01:58.555 --> 00:01:59.930
This is the part
of the API where

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.810
you pass in events from users
interacting with the search,

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.270
so the machine-learning
models can be fine-tuned.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.270
For example, when a user clicks
on a job to see more details,

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.530
you can pass along
a VIEW message.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.260
Once you hook up
the events API, you

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.500
can use the event's live
stream tool in the GCP console

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.110
to make sure that you see
the events as they come in.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.690
In addition, the data
logging terms and conditions

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.600
have been updated, so you can
read exactly how the event

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.170
data is being used.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.310
There's also a new API to create
or update jobs in batches,

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.430
and the client libraries
now support GRPC.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.110
This should make it
easier and give you

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.810
more ways to integrate
the Job Search API.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.550
Since this is all
currently in beta,

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.163
there's still changes coming.

00:02:39.163 --> 00:02:41.330
Make sure to check the
documentation for the latest,

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.077
and please share your feedback.

00:02:43.077 --> 00:02:43.910
Thanks for watching.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:45.525
And remember, when
looking for talent,

00:02:45.525 --> 00:02:47.150
it's OK to keep your
head in the Cloud.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

